  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1020 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nA wet forecast appears to still be on track for mid week. The \naforementioned upper low will eventually track to the southeast and \nbe positioned over the Four Corners by Wednesday with the \nsubtropical ridge remaining in place over the southeastern CONUS. \nTropical moisture from both the Gulf and Pacific will allow for \nPWATs to increase to an average of 1.5 inches by Tuesday. An \nincrease in upper diffluence with the approaching upper low should \nbe enough to at least scattered storm development. The amount of \nconvective coverage will depend on the ultimate track of the upper \nlow. The GFS continues to be more progressive than the ECMWF and, \nwhile it does provide the FA with rainfall, is not as generous as \nthe ECMWF and is much quicker to dryslot the region. The upper low, \nper the GFS, will phase with the main flow by Thursday and will be \nfollowed by the strongest cold front of the season. The ECMWF is \nsticking to its original story of having the upper low stall for a \nvery short period before retrograding and being part of a blocking \npattern. This would be more favorable for more days of rainfall as \nwell as heavier rainfall, but the main story line has yet to be \nfully convincing. While a cold front does move into the FA ahead of \nthe upper low, the building upper ridge into Canada would prevent \nmuch in the way of strong CAA and would instead push a highly \nmodified airmass into the FA as opposed to the GFS's quasi-\nmeridional upper flow.",
